AB  - Digital transformation in agriculture is often hindered by stakeholders’ reluctance to share sensitive operational data. While regulatory pressures for environmental sustainability increase, farmers lack tools to demonstrate compliance without relinquishing control over their data. This paper presents AgrospAI, an agricultural data space demonstrator that is designed to ensure data sovereignty by design. Unlike traditional platforms that require data transfer, AgrospAI utilizes a “Compute-to-Data” (C2D) paradigm. We first demonstrate this via a high-value use case in the Spanish pig farming sector: a sovereign workflow for environmental certification. This system enables farmers to generate verifiable compliance reports while keeping raw sensor data localized and private. Additionally, we showcase a second use case: an on-demand semantic mapping that lowers entry barriers for heterogeneous data sources, allowing data to be shared as-is and mapped and integrated when needed. Our results indicate that AgrospAI provides an environment designed to support trust in which data providers remain sovereign, even when integrating data from diverse sources, and consumers receive actionable insights (e.g., sustainability scores) without ever accessing the providers’ underlying raw datasets. This helps mitigate the risks of data leakage and unauthorized reuse, may help address some of the barriers that hinder data sharing, and enables value generation from data that would otherwise remain unused.
